.TH EIAFS 4
.SH NAME
eiafs \- serial port file server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eiafs
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Eiafs
opens
.B /dev/eia0
(see
.IR uart (3))
for reading and writing, which diverts the port's raw stream to it -- while
it holds the file open, the kernel's own line discipline sees nothing from
the port -- and serves the wire as one file,
.BR eia0 ,
posted at
.BR /srv/eiafs .
It takes no arguments.
.PP
A read of
.B eia0
answers whatever has arrived and not yet been read, and parks when nothing
has.
A write of
.B eia0
goes down the shared descriptor and out the wire; it never parks, because
the UART takes bytes as fast as its FIFO drains.
The device expands a line feed to a carriage return and line feed on the
way out, so the stream through
.B eia0
is a terminal's, not a modem's, and the kernel's own log writes the same
wire unserialised, so the order of a log line against a served write is
not defined.
.PP
The last close of
.B /dev/eia0
gives the port back to the kernel's own line discipline.
The server stops on the pipe's hang-up, when the last mount and the
posted name are both gone.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /srv/eiafs
the posted service
.TP
.B /eia0
the port, read and written
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/eiafs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR ring (2),
.IR ioproc (2),
.IR thread (2),
.IR uart (3),
.IR read (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Eiafs
exits with a number rather than the empty-string convention other
programs use.
Failing to open
.B /dev/eia0
ends it with 116, before it posts anything.
